exports[`screen: 'help api' > en 40 color 1`] = `
"  bladecp api <path>
 ━━━━━━━━━━━━──────────────────────────
  Sends one request to the Customer API
  with the key you signed in with, and
  prints the answer as JSON. Reaches
  every route, including the ones
  without a command of their own.

  Usage ────────────────────────────────
  bladecp api <path> [-X <method>]
      [--data <json> | --input <file>]
      [--yes]

  Arguments ───────────────────────────
  <path>   The route under /api/v1,
           starting with /customer/ (a
           query string is allowed).

  Options ─────────────────────────────
  -X, --method <method>
    HTTP method: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH
    or DELETE
  --data <json>
    JSON body of the request
  --input <file>
    File with the JSON body ("-" reads
    stdin)
  -y, --yes
    Do not ask before a DELETE on a
    terminal

  The method is GET, or POST when you
  send a body. DELETE asks first on a
  terminal; --yes skips the question.
  The key is sent only to the BladeCP
  API and never shown.

  Examples ────────────────────────────
  › bladecp api /customer/me
    The account and key in use
  › bladecp api
    "/customer/provisions?status=active"
    Active servers only (quote a path
    with "?" in the shell)
  › bladecp api -X POST
    /customer/provisions/<uuid>/domains
    --data
    '{"domain":"blog.example.com"}'
    Add a domain to a server
  › bladecp api -X PATCH
    /customer/webhook-endpoints/<id>
    --input endpoint.json
    Send the body from a file ("-"
    reads it from stdin)

  Global options ──────────────────────
  --json
    Print machine-readable JSON (no
    colors, no prompts)
  --api-url <url>
    API address (default
    https://api.bladecp.com)
  --lang <en|pt-BR|es>
    Language for messages
  --debug
    Describe each request on stderr
    (the key is never shown)
  --verbose
    Same as --debug
  -h, --help
    Show help
  -v, --version
    Show the version

  See also  bladecp api list

